WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:10.560
So what is exactly where we use where in order to filter our data based on a condition, and any data

00:10.560 --> 00:16.640
that fulfill the condition can stay in the output, in the result, and the data that don't meet the

00:16.640 --> 00:19.000
condition will be filtered out of the results.

00:19.000 --> 00:20.400
Condition could be anything like.

00:20.400 --> 00:23.760
For example, we say the score must be higher than 500.

00:23.800 --> 00:26.720
Or you can say the country must be equal to Germany.

00:26.720 --> 00:30.560
So any condition that you have in your question now let's see the syntax in SQL.

00:30.600 --> 00:32.240
As usual we start with a select.

00:32.240 --> 00:34.000
We select the columns that we need.

00:34.040 --> 00:36.560
Then we write from where the data come from.

00:36.560 --> 00:42.120
And then after the from we can write the where and exactly after that you specify your condition.

00:42.120 --> 00:44.320
So now let's see how SQL can execute this.

00:44.320 --> 00:46.640
First SQL starts as usual from the from.

00:46.640 --> 00:49.240
So it can go and get your data from the database.

00:49.240 --> 00:53.480
And after that SQL is going to go and execute the Where clause.

00:53.480 --> 00:56.400
So let's say that the condition should be higher than 500.

00:56.440 --> 01:01.700
And now what can happen SQL is going to check each row whether it meets this condition or not.

01:01.700 --> 01:07.580
So for example, for Maria, she doesn't fulfill the condition because her score, the 350, is not

01:07.580 --> 01:14.260
higher than 500, so she doesn't fulfill the condition and skill can go and remove completely this row,

01:14.300 --> 01:16.180
this record from the results.

01:16.220 --> 01:18.060
Now skill can go to the second record.

01:18.060 --> 01:22.060
So John is fulfilling the condition so he can stay in the result.

01:22.060 --> 01:23.340
The same thing for George.

01:23.380 --> 01:25.780
Now moving on to the fourth one Martin.

01:25.780 --> 01:31.220
So this customer is not fulfilling the condition and skill can go and remove it from the results.

01:31.220 --> 01:33.140
The same thing happens for the last customer.

01:33.180 --> 01:35.460
The score is zero and not fulfilling the condition.

01:35.460 --> 01:41.300
So that means if we apply this filter, SQL can return only two customers out of five.

01:41.340 --> 01:46.380
So with that, we are filtering the rows based on condition using the Where clause.

01:46.420 --> 01:48.980
Now, as you can see in the result, we are getting all the columns.

01:48.980 --> 01:54.620
But if you specify in the query like for example, only two columns like the name and the country,

01:54.620 --> 01:58.540
then SQL can start removing as well the columns of the results.

01:58.540 --> 02:02.830
And this means in the output we will get only two columns and two rows.

02:02.830 --> 02:06.790
So with that you are filtering the columns and the rows of your results.

02:06.830 --> 02:09.550
So now let's go back to your SQL in order to practice this.

02:09.590 --> 02:09.990
All right.

02:09.990 --> 02:11.230
So let's have the following task.

02:11.230 --> 02:15.590
And it says retrieve customers with score not equal to zero.

02:15.590 --> 02:18.910
So now if you are looking to our task you see we have like here a condition.

02:18.910 --> 02:22.190
The condition says the score must not be equal to zero.

02:22.190 --> 02:24.230
So I don't want to see all the customers.

02:24.230 --> 02:27.710
I want to see only the customers that fulfill this condition.

02:27.710 --> 02:29.590
So it's like we have to filter the data.

02:29.590 --> 02:30.910
So let's go and solve the task.

02:30.910 --> 02:31.990
Let's start as usual.

02:31.990 --> 02:32.990
Select star.

02:33.030 --> 02:37.790
There is no specifications about the columns from our table customers okay.

02:37.830 --> 02:38.950
So I'm going to start with this.

02:38.990 --> 02:40.230
Let's go and execute it.

02:40.270 --> 02:45.190
Now if you look to the result you can see like almost all the customers are fulfilling their condition.

02:45.190 --> 02:47.390
Their scores are not equal to zero.

02:47.430 --> 02:48.310
Only one.

02:48.350 --> 02:50.430
The last customer his score is zero.

02:50.430 --> 02:52.830
So this customer does not fulfill our condition.

02:52.830 --> 02:54.790
Now let's go and build a filter for that.

02:54.790 --> 02:55.990
So we're going to say where.

02:56.030 --> 03:01.310
And now there will be a section that is only focusing on how to build conditions and filtering in SQL.

03:01.310 --> 03:03.890
So don't worry a lot about the syntax of the conditions.

03:03.890 --> 03:06.490
We're going to cover that later, of course, but it is very simple.

03:06.530 --> 03:08.250
Now for the condition we need a column.

03:08.250 --> 03:10.610
So in which column is our condition based on.

03:10.610 --> 03:11.730
It's going to be on the score.

03:11.730 --> 03:13.370
So we're going to write here score.

03:13.410 --> 03:18.970
And since we are saying not equal there is like an operator in SQL called not equal, then we have to

03:19.010 --> 03:20.090
write a value after that.

03:20.090 --> 03:21.010
So it's going to be a zero.

03:21.010 --> 03:22.770
So again the condition is like this.

03:22.770 --> 03:25.450
The score must not be equal to zero.

03:25.490 --> 03:26.490
It's very simple right.

03:26.490 --> 03:28.410
And with that we have our condition.

03:28.410 --> 03:31.290
And we are using the where in order to filter the data.

03:31.290 --> 03:32.890
So let's go and execute it.

03:32.890 --> 03:38.410
And now as you can see SQL did remove the last customer because he is not fulfilling this condition.

03:38.410 --> 03:42.250
And we have now only the rows that fulfill our condition.

03:42.250 --> 03:44.690
So as you can see it is very simple how to filter the data.

03:44.730 --> 03:50.010
All what you have to do is to write where clause after the from and then write a condition after that.

03:50.050 --> 03:51.210
Now let's have another task.

03:51.210 --> 03:54.690
Like for example, it says retrieve customers from Germany.

03:54.690 --> 03:57.490
So I don't want to see all customers from different countries.

03:57.490 --> 04:00.290
I just want to see the customers that come from Germany.

04:00.290 --> 04:02.340
So that means we have a condition here.

04:02.380 --> 04:05.420
Country of the customer must be equal to Germany.

04:05.420 --> 04:07.620
So let's go and remove the current condition.

04:07.620 --> 04:09.780
It is not the one that we need and executes.

04:09.780 --> 04:14.940
If you are looking to the results, we have two customers that come from Germany and we are interested

04:14.940 --> 04:16.780
only to show those two customers.

04:16.780 --> 04:19.020
So let's go and make a filter for that.

04:19.020 --> 04:23.740
We're going to write where clause and after that we need a column, the column going to be the country.

04:23.740 --> 04:25.340
So we're going to write here country.

04:25.460 --> 04:28.900
And this time the country must be equal to Germany.

04:28.900 --> 04:30.900
So we can write an equal operator.

04:30.900 --> 04:33.060
So we're going to write Germany like this.

04:33.100 --> 04:35.380
Exactly like the value inside our data.

04:35.380 --> 04:38.020
But now as you can see, we are getting like an error here.

04:38.020 --> 04:43.580
And that's because in SQL, if you want to write a value that contains characters, then you have to

04:43.620 --> 04:45.500
put it between two single quotes.

04:45.500 --> 04:48.780
So at the start you put a single quote and as well at the end.

04:48.820 --> 04:52.340
And now as you can see, the red line is away and the value now is red.

04:52.340 --> 04:54.980
And that's because it is a string value.

04:54.980 --> 04:57.140
It is a value that contains characters.

04:57.140 --> 04:58.820
And with that you will not get an error.

04:58.820 --> 05:03.960
So if your columns contains only numbers, you can write it without single quotes, but if your values

05:03.960 --> 05:07.200
contains characters, then you have to write it between two single quotes.

05:07.240 --> 05:08.880
Okay, so now back to our condition.

05:08.920 --> 05:11.080
The country must be equal to Germany.

05:11.080 --> 05:12.440
Let's go and execute it.

05:12.440 --> 05:13.480
And it is working.

05:13.480 --> 05:18.440
So as you can see now we are seeing in the output only the customers that fulfill my condition where

05:18.440 --> 05:20.320
the country is equal to Germany.

05:20.320 --> 05:24.880
So this is exactly how we work with the Where clause in order to filter our data.

05:24.880 --> 05:27.120
So my friends, this is how you filter your rows.

05:27.160 --> 05:30.760
And now let's say that I would like to filter the rows together with the columns.

05:30.760 --> 05:33.400
So I just want to keep the first name and the country.

05:33.400 --> 05:36.040
I'm not interested to see the scores and the IDs.

05:36.040 --> 05:40.840
So in order to do that we're going to go to the select and list the columns that we want to see.

05:40.840 --> 05:44.360
So the first name and after that a comma then the country.

05:44.360 --> 05:45.520
And that's it.

05:45.520 --> 05:48.240
So let's go and give it a push and execute it.

05:48.240 --> 05:50.720
So we have two rows and two columns.

05:50.720 --> 05:53.400
So guys as you can see SQL is very simple.

05:53.440 --> 05:53.800
All right.

05:53.800 --> 05:57.480
So with that you have learned how to filter your data using the Where clause.

05:57.520 --> 06:02.440
Next we're going to talk about how to sort your data using the order by.

06:02.480 --> 06:03.200
So let's go.
